## Summary

Local control automation can request repeated clicks or a drag, receive a successful reply, and leave a pointer queue whose allocation grows directly with the requested count or step number. The handler checks no resource budget before building that queue. Retained verifier observations show bounded queue and resident-memory growth. They do not show process termination or lost work. The existing requirement to cap allocations derived from control parameters applies to this ordinary automation path.

## Steps to reproduce

The fixture below checks ordinary handler reachability and queue observation on the baseline in Runtime or environment. It uses only single and double clicks and a three-step drag. The test sub-agent checked its imports, constructor calls, public queue access, and reply shape against the pinned source, then compiled and executed the exact block unchanged. The single test passed with exit 0 and no failed assertions. It does not measure resident memory or demonstrate the missing budget by increasing input.

In a disposable checkout of that baseline, place the complete block in `crates/ui-egui/tests/pointer_batch_reachability.rs`. Compile only this fixed integration target with `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 nice -n 10 cargo test --locked --offline -p gridcraft-ui-egui --test pointer_batch_reachability --no-run --message-format=json`, using a shared temporary `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`. The test sub-agent then ran the executable identified by Cargo with `nice -n 10 <test-executable> normal_pointer_batches --exact --nocapture --test-threads=1`. The build had a ten-minute deadline and the test had a thirty-second deadline. No frame loop runs in the fixture; it reads the public queue immediately after the handler returns and before any frame can drain it.

