## Summary

ROUNDDOWN and TRUNC return #NUM! when asked to round the nonzero numbers 1234, -1234 or 0.5 toward zero at precision -309 or -400. The documented direction and precision semantics require zero for these inputs. The same functions return zero at precision -308, and zero input succeeds at all three tested precisions.

## Steps to reproduce

Use the source baseline under Runtime or environment. Save this small integration test as crates/functions/tests/rounding_boundary.rs. The calls and results below were executed through the public function API in a focused package test.

```rust
use gridcraft_core::{DateSystem, Value};
use gridcraft_functions::{Arg, Ctx, call, lookup};

struct Context;
impl Ctx for Context {
    fn date_system(&self) -> DateSystem { DateSystem::default() }
    fn now_serial(&self) -> f64 { 0.0 }
    fn random(&mut self) -> f64 { 0.5 }
}

#[test]
fn rounding_boundary() {
    for name in ["ROUNDDOWN", "TRUNC", "ROUND", "ROUNDUP"] {
        for digits in [-308.0, -309.0, -400.0] {
            let args = [Arg::val(1234.0), Arg::val(digits)];
            let result: Value = call(
                lookup(name).expect("registered function"),
                &args,
                &mut Context,
            );
            println!("{name}(1234,{digits}) = {result:?}");
        }
    }
}
```

Run only the focused target:

```sh
CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 cargo test -p gridcraft-functions --test rounding_boundary -- --nocapture
```

Inspect the ROUNDDOWN and TRUNC results at -309 and -400. Repeat their calls with the first argument set to -1234 or 0.5 to observe the same error, and with zero to observe the early-return control.

