**What happens.** When a sheet has a dynamic-array spill (or a legacy CSE array) whose values are text, the XLSX writer saves the cells after the anchor as shared-string constants (`t="s"` with an index into `sharedStrings.xml`). Excel opens such a file with `#VALUE!` in those cells until it recalculates the sheet.

**Where it was seen.** @iluebbe found it while testing #2187 (https://github.com/storytold/gridcraft/issues/2137#issuecomment-6334492459): a workbook saved by Excel for Microsoft 365 with `=SORT({"b";"a";"c"})` in K4, read and saved by GridCraft, shows `#VALUE!` in K5:K6 in Excel until a recalculation; numbers (`=SEQUENCE(3)`) and logicals (`={1;2;3}=2`) in the same workbook were fine. Quoting the finding: "`main` writes the same after a normal open and save (`gridcraft-cli convert`, which recalculates), so this is not caused by the PR". So it is on `main` independently of #2187.

**Cause.** `write_cell` in `crates/xlsx/src/sheet_write.rs` takes the spilled value through the `extra` parameter, and because the cell has no formula of its own it falls through to the plain-value branch, which writes text as a shared string:

```rust
Value::Text(t) => {
    let i = out.sst.index(t);
    let _ = write!(s, " t=\"s\"><v>{i}</v></c>");
}
```

The anchor cell is written correctly (`<f t="array" ref="K4:K6">` with `t="str"`). Only the cells inside the range are affected, and only for text: numbers, booleans and errors use the same cell types in both paths.

**What Excel writes.** In a file saved by Excel, the cells inside a spill range have no `<f>`, and text results carry `t="str"` with the text inline in `<v>`, the same type Excel uses for the string result of a formula cell. (From Excel-saved files; the repository's dynamic-array fixtures are synthetic and only cover numbers, so this case has no fixture yet.)

